Original commit body from deb64293a265799d8bf2bd1876cd84852d13767b:

Second sweep pass. Same mechanism as F1-F3 -- a row minting a taint SOURCE, or
accepting a taint SINK, at a call that moves no application data -- reached
through a different shape: connection lifecycle rather than construction.

REMOVED
  javascript  WebSocket.onopen, WebSocket.onerror, EventSource.onerror
              (net_recv). The open event carries no data and the error event is
              SPECIFIED to carry none -- deliberately, so a cross-origin
              failure cannot leak why it failed.
  haskell     Network.Socket.close (net_send). Closing emits a FIN, which is
              not a payload any line of the program produced, and net_send is a
              taint SINK -- so the row accepted a tainted value as "sent" at a
              call that sends nothing of the program's.
  haskell     Network.Socket.gracefulClose (net_recv). THE SUBTLE ONE: it
              really does read, draining until EOF or timeout, so bytes do
              cross the boundary. It still is not a source -- the function
              returns unit, so not one of those bytes reaches the program, and
              net_recv under the auto-source map means a source of data the
              program then USES.

KEPT, AND THE DISTINCTION IS WHAT MAKES THE REMOVALS DEFENSIBLE
  javascript  WebSocket.onclose. A CloseEvent carries `code` and `reason`, both
              chosen by the peer, so it genuinely delivers peer-controlled data
              into the program. Removing all three "lifecycle" callbacks
              together would have been the tidy answer and the wrong one.

MOVED, NOT DELETED
  javascript  BroadcastChannel.postMessage carried TWO defects in one row: it
              SENDS and was declared net_recv -- a direction error, minting
              untrusted_input at a call that emits -- and the channel is not
              network in either direction, being same-origin messaging between
              browsing contexts where nothing leaves the browser. Both sides
              move to ipc_*. Moving keeps the send REPRESENTED; deleting would
              lose a sink, which is the false-all-clear direction. The receive
              label is unchanged because ipc_recv and net_recv both derive
              untrusted_input.

EVERY REMOVAL PASSES THE SAME RULE AS F1-F3: the channel's real transfer is
still catalogued. WebSocket keeps onmessage/addEventListener/send, EventSource
keeps onmessage, Network.Socket keeps recv/recvFrom and send/sendTo/sendAll/
sendMany. Asserted as controls in the same tests.

A STRANDING CHECK NOW SITS AT THE POINT OF REMOVAL. PR #602 removed the only
function-kind row from two modules, leaving them method-only and therefore
reported method-starved -- which withholds every verdict on a repo -- and it
merged green because a hand-picked manifest did not name the test that would
have caught it. Each module touched here asserts that its set of call KINDS is
unchanged, in the same file as the removal, rather than relying on a test
elsewhere that this change might again fail to select.

